<p>The most interesting work I know is made by small teams with a long horizon. Not because small is morally superior, but because smallness makes it possible to hold the whole thing in your head.</p>

<p>When a team is small, decisions are cheap. You can try something on Monday and undo it on Wednesday. You can disagree without forming a committee. You can care about details that would be invisible to a larger organization.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>A long horizon is not a promise to work slowly. It is a promise not to make every decision as if this quarter were the only one.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>The combination is rare because it is structurally difficult. Small teams are under pressure to grow. Long horizons are under pressure to produce. The culture that sustains both has to be built deliberately, and defended more than once.</p>

<p>The reward is a different kind of progress. You get to make things that are coherent, that accumulate, that people can trust. You get to be here next year. That is not a small thing.</p>
